### 4.12 `cargo-build-system` (Expanded)

This variable is exported by `(guix build-system cargo)`. It supports builds of packages using Cargo, the build tool of the Rust programming language.

**Deprecation Warning**: Parameters `#:cargo-inputs` and `#:cargo-development-inputs`, previously used for specifying source inputs, are deprecated. These keyword parameters are being **replaced** by the `cargo-inputs` procedure, which looks up inputs from the new `(gnu packages rust-crates)` module. This procedural approach is part of a broader effort to manage Rust libraries in dedicated modules (`(gnu packages rust-crates)` and `(gnu packages rust-sources)`), designed to better handle the complex dependency graphs of Rust crates. The `cargo-inputs` procedure accepts an optional `#:module` argument and returns a list of inputs suitable for the `#:cargo-inputs` keyword. *(Note: no firm removal date for the old keyword parameters has been announced; the transition is ongoing as part of the Rust team's work on the lockfile-importer-based workflow.)*

It adds `rust` and `rust:cargo` to the set of inputs. A different Rust package can be specified with the `#:rust` parameter. In its `unpack-rust-crates`, `configure` and `patch-cargo-checksums` phases, this build system will make any source inputs available to Cargo. It will also remove an included `Cargo.lock` file to be recreated by Cargo during the build phase. The `package` phase will run `cargo package` to create a source crate for future use. The `install` phase installs the binaries defined by the crate. Unless `install-source? #f` is defined, it will also install a source crate repository of itself and unpacked sources, to ease in future hacking on Rust packages. This build system supports Cargo workspaces.

Parameter `#:cargo-package-crates` (default: `'()`) allows specifying names of **library crates** to package in the `package` phase. Specified crates are packaged from left to right, in case there's dependency among them. For example, specifying `'("pcre2-sys" "pcre2")` will package `"pcre2-sys"` first and then `"pcre2"`. This parameter is a required parameter when packaging a Cargo workspace dependency.

Parameter `#:cargo-install-paths` (default: `'()`) allows specifying paths of binary crates to install in the `install` phase, `'("crates/atuin")` for example.

**2025 Status**: The Rust team has been actively working on improving Cargo workspace support to automatically detect and install all binary targets, which currently requires manual specification via `#:cargo-install-paths`.

#### Rust Crate Handling in Guix (Expanded)

Rust applications (binary crates) and libraries (library crates) are packaged separately. Guix puts its main efforts into applications and only packages libraries as sources, utilizing automation with a manual focus on unbundling vendored dependencies. Rust applications are treated like any other package and named using the lowercase upstream name. When using the Cargo build system, Rust applications should have the `#:install-source?` parameter set to `#f`, as this parameter only makes sense for libraries. When the package source is a Cargo workspace, `#:cargo-install-paths` must be set to enable relevant support.

Rust libraries are hidden from the user interface and managed in two modules:

- **`(gnu packages rust-crates)`**: Source definitions imported from Rust packages' `Cargo.lock` via the crate importer. Imported definitions must be checked and have vendored dependencies unbundled before being contributed to Guix.
- **`(gnu packages rust-sources)`**: More complex definitions that need to be full packages. This includes Rust libraries requiring external inputs to unbundle and Cargo workspaces. These libraries should have the `#:skip-build?` parameter set to `#t`. For Cargo workspaces, `#:cargo-package-crates` must be set.

Since they are added manually, they follow the usual naming convention for Guix packages, with a `rust-` prefix. In the Rust community it is common for multiple incompatible versions of a package to be used at any given time, so all libraries should have a versioned suffix. The versioned suffix is the left-most non-zero digit (and any leading zeros). This follows the "caret" version scheme intended by Cargo. Examples: `rust-clap-2`, `rust-rand-0.6`. In practice Guix often packages development snapshots of Rust libraries and can't simply identify them by version. In this case, the complete version information can be turned into a version string via `git-version`, for example `rust-pipewire-0.8.0.fd3d8f7` and `rust-pubgrub-0.3.0.b70cf70`.

To avoid merge conflicts with changes from multiple branches, these two modules are maintained by the Rust team. Rust libraries are not referenced directly. `(guix build-system cargo)` provides a `cargo-inputs` procedure to lookup input lists generated by the lockfile importer:

```scheme
(cargo-inputs name [#:module '(gnu packages rust-crates)])
```

Lookup Cargo inputs for `name` in `module`, return an empty list if unavailable. `name` must be consistent with the one used in lockfile importer invocation, usually matching the variable name of the package:

```bash
guix import -i gnu/packages/rust-crates.scm crate -f /path/to/Cargo.lock name
```

`module` must export a public interface `lookup-cargo-inputs`, a template is available in the `etc/teams/rust` directory of Guix source tree.

**Module**: `(guix build-system cargo)`

#### Porting `cargo-build-system`

- **Implicit inputs**: Rust, Cargo. The `#:rust` argument overrides the Rust package.
- **Phase overrides**:
    - `unpack-rust-crates`: Make source inputs available to Cargo.
    - `configure`: Set up the Cargo environment, remove `Cargo.lock` if present.
    - `patch-cargo-checksums`: Patch checksums in `.cargo-checksum.json` files.
    - `build`: Run `cargo build`.
    - `package`: Run `cargo package` for each crate in `#:cargo-package-crates`.
    - `install`: Install binaries (and optionally source) using `cargo install` or by copying from `target/`.
- **Workspace support**: If the package is a Cargo workspace, use `#:cargo-install-paths` to specify which binary crates to install.

In a port, you would implement these phases as wrappers around Cargo commands, with the source inputs made available in the Cargo registry cache.


